1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,729
Two people start on the same day, with the
same skills, and work just as hard.

2
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:06,608
A year later, one is far ahead.

3
00:00:06,608 --> 00:00:08,487
Is that unfair, or is it maths?

4
00:00:08,487 --> 00:00:11,397
Researchers have found patterns in who
makes it.

5
00:00:11,397 --> 00:00:12,246
Here are five.

6
00:00:12,848 --> 00:00:16,353
First: the same start doesn't mean the
same outcome.

7
00:00:16,353 --> 00:00:21,206
In one online experiment, 14,341 people
could download unfamiliar songs.

8
00:00:21,206 --> 00:00:23,835
When they could see what others picked,

9
00:00:23,835 --> 00:00:29,295
the same songs ended up with very
different levels of success in separate
groups.

10
00:00:29,295 --> 00:00:33,070
Quality still mattered: the best songs
rarely did badly.

11
00:00:33,711 --> 00:00:35,709
Second: small advantages compound.

12
00:00:35,709 --> 00:00:38,236
Improve by one percent a day and, on
paper,

13
00:00:38,236 --> 00:00:41,703
a year multiplies where you started by
almost thirty-eight.

14
00:00:41,703 --> 00:00:45,228
Slip one percent a day and you keep less
than three percent.

15
00:00:45,228 --> 00:00:47,814
Real life is messier, but the shape is
real.

16
00:00:47,814 --> 00:00:51,046
Sociologist Robert Merton called it the
Matthew effect:

17
00:00:51,046 --> 00:00:55,688
scientists who are already recognised tend
to get more credit for similar work.

18
00:00:56,299 --> 00:00:58,695
Third: rewards aren't shared out evenly.

19
00:00:58,695 --> 00:01:03,486
Economist Sherwin Rosen showed how, when
one performer can reach a whole market,

20
00:01:03,486 --> 00:01:06,780
a small edge in talent can mean a huge gap
in earnings.

21
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:11,093
And a study of more than six hundred
thousand researchers, entertainers,

22
00:01:11,093 --> 00:01:15,106
politicians and athletes found that
performance itself is lopsided:

23
00:01:15,106 --> 00:01:18,220
a small group at the top far outpaces
everyone else.

24
00:01:18,859 --> 00:01:20,538
Fourth: luck compounds too.

25
00:01:20,538 --> 00:01:25,388
One computer model of forty-year careers
found the most successful people were

26
00:01:25,388 --> 00:01:26,943
rarely the most talented,

27
00:01:26,943 --> 00:01:29,181
but moderately talented and luckier.

28
00:01:29,181 --> 00:01:31,295
It's a simulation, not real lives.

29
00:01:31,295 --> 00:01:35,710
One possible lesson: be prepared, so a
lucky break has somewhere to go.

30
00:01:36,362 --> 00:01:37,778
Fifth: aim your effort.

31
00:01:37,778 --> 00:01:38,701
Taken together,

32
00:01:38,701 --> 00:01:43,934
this research suggests hard work pays most
when it goes into skills and markets where

33
00:01:43,934 --> 00:01:45,534
gains build on themselves.

34
00:01:45,534 --> 00:01:50,644
Choosing the field, the skill and the
thing you build matters as much as the
hours.

35
00:01:51,249 --> 00:01:54,300
So effort matters, but it isn't the whole
equation.

36
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:57,591
Compounding, unequal rewards and luck
shape the result.

37
00:01:57,591 --> 00:02:00,702
The part you control is where you point
your effort.
